Key business assumptions:

a) The API is internal-only. Clients need existing VPN, peering, or private network connectivity.
b) The assessment accepts a costed design without live AWS deployment.
c) The API has modest, simple workload requirements: JSON validation and no long-running processing.
d) mTLS authenticates the caller identity, but application-level authorization is not included.
e) Availability across two AZs is sufficient; a multi-region or disaster-recovery design is not required.
f) One AWS account and one dev environment are sufficient.
g) The default VPC CIDR will not conflict with connected corporate networks.
h) Log retention of 30 days is sufficient for the assessment.
i) Test certificates are acceptable; a real domain and commercial certificates are not required.
j) SNS notification is a demonstration; an operational alerting subscription is not required yet.

Technical debt:

a) Terraform state contains generated CA, server, and client private keys.
Production certificate lifecycle is not implemented: issuance, rotation, revocation, and private DNS remain outstanding.
b) Lambda HTTPS egress allows the full VPC CIDR instead of endpoint-specific destinations.
c) Custom NACLs and VPC Flow Logs are not implemented.
d) The S3 gateway endpoint is not used by the current Lambda.
e) ALB access and connection logs are not centrally forwarded or immutable.
f) Security-group rejects are not visible in ALB logs.
g) KMS protects the SSM parameter but not all log/S3 data.
h) No SNS subscriber, pager, or runbook is configured.
i) No remote Terraform backend, DynamoDB locking, or environment/account state separation exists.
k) The GitHub OIDC provider, AWS plan role, and live merge rules are not account-verified.
l) The CI plan uses backend=false; it cannot detect deployed-state drift.
m) The workflow does not deploy or destroy infrastructure.
n) No live AWS evidence exists: mTLS, ALB health, logs, alarm delivery, and billing remain unverified.
o) The /health check tests Lambda liveness but not SSM/KMS dependency readiness.
mTLS does not provide application-level authorization.